.TH CYAML_SERIALIZE 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_serialize \- serialize a DOM tree to block-style YAML
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cyaml.h>

char *cyaml_serialize(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Produce a human-readable, block-style YAML document from the given DOM
subtree.  Scalars are emitted as plain text when safe (i.e., when they
would round-trip without implicit type ambiguity); otherwise they are
double-quoted.  Lists use "- value" block notation.  Dictionaries use
"key: value" block notation.  Nested structures are indented by two
spaces per level.

A dictionary's own key order in the output is unspecified: it follows
this DOM's internal storage order, which is unrelated to insertion
order, parse order, or any other caller-visible ordering, so a
parse-then-serialize round trip is not guaranteed to reproduce the
source document's own key order. A list's element order, by
contrast, is always exactly the order its elements were pushed or
parsed in. A caller needing a specific, stable key order in
serialized output must arrange for it itself (e.g. by re-inserting
keys into a fresh dictionary in the desired order immediately before
serializing).

A node's tag (see cyaml_node_tag(3)) is emitted as a leading
"!<...>" verbatim tag whenever it is a custom tag, or a core-schema
tag (CYAML_TAG_NULL, _BOOL, _INT, _FLOAT, _STR, _SEQ, or _MAP) that
does not actually match the node's own type; a core-schema tag that
does match its node's type is never emitted, since the surrounding
quoting/formatting rules already guarantee the value round-trips to
that same type without it.  A mismatched core-schema tag (only
reachable via cyaml_node_set_tag(3), which permits attaching one to a
node of a different type) is therefore never silently lost.

The returned buffer is allocated using the root node's stored allocator.
It must be freed with cyaml_serialize_free() (or cyaml_serialize_free_mp()
if a custom allocator was used).

node need not have come from cyaml_parse*(); a tree built directly via
cyaml_create_list_mp(3)/cyaml_dictionary_set(3) is subject to the same
500-level nesting limit cyaml_parse*(3) enforces at parse time. If such
a tree is nested past that limit, this function returns NULL rather than
recursing without bound.

A NULL node serializes as if it were a CYAML_NULL node ("~\en"); this
is a successful, non-NULL return, not a failure signal (unlike
cyaml_clone(3), which returns NULL for a NULL node).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Heap-allocated null-terminated YAML string on success, NULL on OOM or if
node is nested more than 500 levels deep (the two failure causes are
indistinguishable, since this function has no separate error-string
channel).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_serialize_flow (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free_mp (3),
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3)
